\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Measurement error.}
Classical theory shows correction via estimated error models can amplify MSE under misspecification~\cite{fuller1987measurement,carroll2006measurement}.
We study effect modifier misclassification---the mismeasured variable defines the CATE function---rather than regressor~\cite{frazis2003estimating} or confounder~\cite{ogburn2012nondifferential} error.
\citet{frazis2003estimating} derive scalar attenuation $\lambda = (1-2\pi)$ for homogeneous errors; Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity} generalizes this to subgroup-heterogeneous settings where correction can reverse bias sign (Appendix~\ref{app:fl_comparison}).

\paragraph{HTE estimation and LLM annotation.}
Causal forests~\cite{athey2016recursive,wager2018causal}, debiased ML~\cite{chernozhukov2018dml}, and meta-learners~\cite{kunzel2019metalearners} assume covariates defining heterogeneity are observed without error.
LLMs now match crowd-worker accuracy~\cite{gilardi2023chatgpt,tornberg2024chatgpt}, but error rates vary by up to $5\times$ across subgroups~\cite{ashwin2025bias}.
\citet{wang2020methods} study inference correction when outcomes are ML-predicted, but assume the covariate defining heterogeneity is measured without error.

\paragraph{Prediction-powered and design-based correction.}
PPI~\cite{angelopoulos2023ppi,zrnic2024cross} and DSL~\cite{egami2023dsl} correct population-level aggregates assuming homogeneous prediction error; pooled DSL is equivalent to our Global baseline (Appendix~\ref{app:ppi_comparison}).
\citet{mozer2026ppi} extend PPI to arm-specific ATE; PPCI~\cite{sui2026ppci} corrects outcome-side error; ForestIV~\cite{bu2023forestiv} targets the outcome model.
None addresses bias when the mismeasured variable is the effect modifier indexing the CATE function.
Extended discussion appears in Appendix~\ref{app:related}.

